End the ConPTY read loop on any unexpected error

The reader thread's loop looks like a spin at a glance -- a while (true) with no
sleep -- but it is not, in the normal case: ReadFile on a blocking pipe parks in
the syscall until bytes arrive. Measured on an idle MSYS2 shell it costs 0.000s
of CPU over 10s, and 0.000s over 22s after the shell has exited.

The error path is another matter, and it is not hypothetical. kill() walks into
it on every teardown, because it closes the pipe the reader is blocked on before
it asks the thread to stop:

    closePseudoConsole(m_ptyHandler);
    CloseHandle(m_hPipeOut);
    CloseHandle(m_hPipeIn);                 // the reader's ReadFile fails here
    m_readThread->requestInterruption();    // ... the flag is only set now

So ReadFile fails on a closed handle at a moment when isInterruptionRequested()
is still false. Of the ways it can fail, exactly two were handled:
ERROR_MORE_DATA appended what it got, ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE broke out. Anything else
-- ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED, or the ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE of the case above --
appended nothing, did not break, and did not block, so the loop went straight
back into ReadFile to fail the same way again.

Be clear about the size of it: the spin is bounded by the gap to
requestInterruption(), microseconds rather than a pegged core, and no sustained
busy-loop was ever reproduced. The sharper problem is what it spins on. Once
CloseHandle returns, that HANDLE value is free for any other thread to receive
from CreateFile, and re-entering ReadFile on it reads from whatever object
inherited the number.

Invert the test: append on success or ERROR_MORE_DATA, break on anything else.
Broken pipe is still covered, now as the ordinary case of "this pipe is
finished" rather than as the one error worth noticing, and the interruption
check keeps its own break.

GetLastError() is also captured once instead of being called twice for two
different questions. It is thread-global state; reading it twice around
intervening work is a bug waiting for a maintainer.

0010 fixes the ordering in kill() that leads here. This patch is still worth
having on its own: it bounds the damage to one failed read whatever the caller
does.

Origin: this loop arrives with 0001, from ptyqt, not from qtermwidget upstream.

Upstream: not submitted; belongs on PR #578, which is not being merged --
         see 0001's header
Applies-to: all environments (the changed code is behind Q_OS_WIN)

--- a/lib/ptyqt/conptyprocess.cpp
+++ b/lib/ptyqt/conptyprocess.cpp
@@ -202,15 +202,27 @@
             // Read from the pipe
             BOOL result = ReadFile(m_hPipeIn, szBuffer, BUFF_SIZE, &dwBytesRead, NULL);
 
-            const bool needMoreData = !result && GetLastError() == ERROR_MORE_DATA;
+            // Capture the error once. GetLastError() was previously consulted
+            // twice for two different questions, which is fragile: it is
+            // thread-global state and anything in between could reset it.
+            const DWORD lastError = result ? ERROR_SUCCESS : GetLastError();
+
+            const bool needMoreData = !result && lastError == ERROR_MORE_DATA;
             if (result || needMoreData) {
                 QMutexLocker locker(&m_bufferMutex);
                 m_buffer.m_readBuffer.append(szBuffer, dwBytesRead);
                 m_buffer.emitReadyRead();
+            } else {
+                // Any other failure ends the loop. Only ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE used
+                // to, so an unexpected error -- ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED or
+                // ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE after the handle is closed underneath
+                // this thread -- fell through to the bottom of the loop, did
+                // not break, and did not block: ReadFile would fail again
+                // immediately, spinning a core until the object was destroyed.
+                break;
             }
 
-            const bool brokenPipe = !result && GetLastError() == ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE;
-            if (QThread::currentThread()->isInterruptionRequested() || brokenPipe)
+            if (QThread::currentThread()->isInterruptionRequested())
                 break;
         }
     });
